# Copy to deploy/server.env (git-ignored) and fill in. Read by `npm run server:setup | server:deploy | server:backup`.

# The server, reached with `ssh SERVER_USER@SERVER_HOST` using your ssh key. The user must be root: setup installs
# Docker and a systemd timer.
SERVER_HOST=
SERVER_USER=root
# Where the studio lives on the server (default /opt/flowchain).
# SERVER_DIR=/opt/flowchain

# The studio's public name. Its DNS record must point at the server before setup: the HTTPS certificate is
# issued for this name. Without accounts (below), the proxy asks for one login: its password is generated by
# setup and shown once.
STUDIO_HOST=
STUDIO_USER=studio

# How many generations run at the same time (default 2).
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

# Nightly copy of runs, brand kits and uploads to a Cloudflare R2 bucket. Leave empty for no backup. Use a bucket
# of its own, not the one RunPod's files pass through. The R2 account and keys are taken from .env unless given
# here as BACKUP_R2_ACCOUNT_ID, BACKUP_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID and BACKUP_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY.
BACKUP_BUCKET=

# Accounts (optional). With STUDIO_ACCOUNTS=1 the studio has accounts: visitors sign up and sign in at the
# studio, each sees only their own videos, and paid work comes out of their credit. The proxy's single login
# (STUDIO_USER) is then not used. The accounts live in a database of the studio's own, which setup starts on
# the server and whose passwords it makes and keeps there. Needs a mail server (the studio confirms every
# address by email), and should have a bucket. These can be set here or in .env:
# STUDIO_ACCOUNTS=1
# SMTP_URL=smtps://<user>:<password>@<smtp host>      any SMTP service
# MAIL_FROM=Flow Chain AI <hello@yourdomain>
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=         optional: "Continue with Google" (redirect URI https://<STUDIO_HOST>/auth/callback)
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Publishing to users' own YouTube channels (optional; needs STUDIO_ACCOUNTS=1; all three or none).
# An OAuth client of a Google Cloud project with the YouTube Data API v3 on (redirect URI
# https://<STUDIO_HOST>/auth/youtube/callback). Until Google has audited the project, what it uploads stays private.
# YOUTUBE_CLIENT_ID=
# YOUTUBE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# CHANNEL_KEY=              32 random bytes in base64 (openssl rand -base64 32). Channel tokens are sealed with it:
#                           keep it, and never change it, or every user must connect their channel again.
# STUDIO_BUCKET=            a private R2 bucket of its own for runs and uploads (not the one RunPod's files pass through)
# STUDIO_USER_JOBS=2        how many long jobs one account may have waiting or working
# STUDIO_CACHE_DAYS=14      how long a stored run stays on the server's disk untouched

# Payments (optional, needs accounts). With STRIPE_SECRET_KEY set — here or in .env — users buy credit themselves
# at /pricing. Put the key in .env, describe what you sell in billing/plans.json, and run `npm run stripe:setup`:
# it creates the products, the portal and the webhook in Stripe and writes STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET to .env.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=            sk_test_... to begin with (test mode); a live key (sk_live_...) takes real money

# Scene sound (README, "Scene sound") needs no setting of its own: a scene's sound is its LTX clip's, offered while the worker has
# FLOWCHAIN_LTX=on. RUNPOD_FOLEY_ENDPOINT and FLOWCHAIN_SCENE_SOUND, of the scene-sound worker removed on 2026-11-02, are read by nothing.

# LTX clips and speaking characters (README, "Clips with LTX"). These are the worker's, taken from .env. New runs
# have moving clips only while FLOWCHAIN_LTX=on, RUNPOD_LTX_ENDPOINT is set and the three runpodLtx* prices are in prices.json;
# otherwise only videos of pictures can be made (Wan, which made clips before, was removed on 2026-11-02). Set FLOWCHAIN_LTX=on here only after the licence answer and the approved terms, then run
# npm run server:setup. To roll back, set FLOWCHAIN_LTX=off here (runs already made on LTX still finish on it).
# RUNPOD_LTX_ENDPOINT=          written to .env by npm run runpod:deploy
# FLOWCHAIN_LTX=off             on or off (default off)
# FLOWCHAIN_SPEECH=off          on or off (default off); a character speaks only while LTX is on as well

# The provider keys and settings are taken from this machine's .env. Anything set below replaces or adds to
# them for the server only; a name set to nothing keeps this machine's value off the server. For example:
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-flash-latest
